    "q-95340": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95340",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${A}^{T}=A$\n\nII. ${A}^{2}=A$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal projection matrix",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if ${Q}^{2}=Q$ and ${Q}^{T}=Q.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${A}^{T}=A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}20 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{20}{9} & \\frac{10}{9} \\\\ \\frac{10}{9} & \\frac{5}{9}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since not both statements I and II are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
